def-inner-product-space.interface:
## Definition

Let $F$ be either $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, with conjugation equal to the identity on $\mathbb R$ and with complex conjugation as in [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]. An **inner product** on an $F$-vector space $V$ is a function $\langle\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,\rangle:V\times V\to F$ such that for all $u,v,w\in V$ and $a,b\in F$:

1. $\langle au+bv,w\rangle=a\langle u,w\rangle+b\langle v,w\rangle$;
2. $\langle u,v\rangle=\overline{\langle v,u\rangle}$;
3. $\langle v,v\rangle$ is real and nonnegative, and $\langle v,v\rangle=0$ if and only if $v=0$.

The first two clauses imply conjugate-linearity in the second argument: $\langle u,av+bw\rangle=\overline a\langle u,v\rangle+\overline b\langle u,w\rangle$. This is the linear-first convention of [[def-sesquilinear-and-hermitian-forms-over-a-field-with-involution]]. A vector space equipped with an inner product is an **inner product space**.

def-spectral-network-complexity.interface:
## Definition

Let $L\ge1$ and let $d_0,\ldots,d_L\ge1$ be layer widths, with $K:=d_L\ge2$ output classes. For each $i\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$, fix a matrix $M_i\in\mathbb R^{d_i\times d_{i-1}}$ and a vector activation $\sigma_i:\mathbb R^{d_i}\to\mathbb R^{d_i}$ satisfying $\sigma_i(0)=0$ and
$$\lVert\sigma_i(u)-\sigma_i(v)\rVert_2\le\rho_i\lVert u-v\rVert_2\qquad(u,v\in\mathbb R^{d_i})$$
for a fixed $\rho_i>0$. A network has matrices $A_i\in\mathbb R^{d_i\times d_{i-1}}$, no biases, and is evaluated by $h_0(x)=x$ and $h_i(x)=\sigma_i(A_i h_{i-1}(x))$, with score vector $F_A(x)=h_L(x)$. For a real matrix $B$, define $\lVert B\rVert_{2,1}:=\sum_j\lVert B_{\cdot j}\rVert_2$, the sum of the Euclidean norms of its columns. Write $\lVert A\rVert_\sigma$ for the induced Euclidean operator norm $\max_{\lVert u\rVert_2=1}\lVert Au\rVert_2$ and put $W:=\max_{0\le i\le L}d_i$.

For a network whose layer matrices all have nonzero operator norm, its **spectral network complexity relative to $(M_i)$** is
$$R_A:=\left(\prod_{i=1}^L\rho_i\lVert A_i\rVert_\sigma\right)\left(\sum_{i=1}^L\left(\frac{\lVert A_i^\top-M_i^\top\rVert_{2,1}}{\lVert A_i\rVert_\sigma}\right)^{2/3}\right)^{3/2}.$$
If some $A_i=0$, the network is instead handled as the constant-zero score map: the recurrence gives $h_i=0$ and all later hidden vectors are zero. The displayed complexity is not defined in that zero-layer case.

The block diagram is schematic for $L\ge3$ and depicts the stated recurrence. Each node is the entire vector at one layer; the arrow labelled $A_i$ is the zero-bias linear map, followed by the vector activation shown in the next node. The omitted middle layers are represented by the ellipsis, and the widths are those in the definition. For $L=1$ or $L=2$, omit the ellipsis and use the corresponding initial segment of the recurrence. The diagram is illustrative and is not used in any proof.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \node[draw,rounded corners] (h0) at (0,0) {$h_0=x\in\mathbb R^{d_0}$};
  \node[draw,rounded corners] (h1) at (3.1,0) {$h_1=\sigma_1(A_1h_0)$};
  \node (dots) at (4.9,0) {$\cdots$};
  \node[draw,rounded corners] (hL) at (6.8,0) {$h_L=F_A(x)$};
  \draw[->] (h0) -- node[above] {$A_1$} (h1);
  \draw[->] (h1) -- (dots);
  \draw[->] (dots) -- node[above] {$A_L$} (hL);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

def-feedforward-neural-network.interface:
## Definition

Fix integers $d,r\ge1$. A finite feedforward computation graph from $\mathbb R^d$ to $\mathbb R^r$ consists of a finite directed graph $(V,E)$ with no directed cycle; pairwise disjoint input, hidden, and output vertex sets $I,H,O$ whose union is $V$; exactly $d$ input vertices and $r$ output vertices; and a specified ordering of $V$ in which the tail of every directed edge precedes its head. The input vertices are labelled bijectively by the coordinates $1,\ldots,d$, and input vertex $i$ has value $x_i$ for $x\in\mathbb R^d$. Each non-input vertex $v$ has a real bias $b_v$, a real weight $w_{uv}$ for every edge $u\to v$, and a real activation function $\rho_v$. In the specified order, its value is

$$z_v=\rho_v\!\left(b_v+\sum_{u:\,u\to v}w_{uv}z_u\right).$$

The network output is the vector of values at $O$. A layered multilayer perceptron is the specialization with layers $V_0,\ldots,V_L$, where $V_0=I$, $V_L=O$, and every edge goes from $V_{\ell-1}$ to $V_\ell$ for some $\ell$; the hidden vertices use a shared activation and the output vertices use the identity activation. A fully connected layered perceptron includes every edge between consecutive layers. The fixed ordering makes the recursive evaluation unambiguous and is part of the graph data.

The diagram shows the edge direction and evaluation convention for a fully connected scalar-output perceptron with one hidden layer. Each circle is one scalar-valued node; the hidden nodes apply $\sigma$ and the output node is linear. The diagram illustrates the definition and is not used in any proof.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.5cm]
  \node[draw,circle] (x1) {$x_1$};
  \node[draw,circle,below of=x1] (x2) {$x_2$};
  \node[draw,circle,right of=x1,yshift=-0.35cm] (h1) {$\sigma$};
  \node[draw,circle,below of=h1] (h2) {$\sigma$};
  \node[draw,circle,right of=h1,yshift=-0.35cm] (y) {$y$};
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (h1);
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (h2);
  \draw[->] (x2) -- (h1);
  \draw[->] (x2) -- (h2);
  \draw[->] (h1) -- (y);
  \draw[->] (h2) -- (y);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

alg-backpropagation-through-shared-param.interface:
## Definition

Let $p,q\ge1$, let $\Theta\subseteq\mathbb R^p$ and $H\subseteq\mathbb R^q$ be open, let $T\ge0$ be an integer, and fix $\theta\in\Theta$. A map is continuously differentiable here when it is totally differentiable at every point of its open domain and its derivative matrix varies continuously there. A finite shared-parameter recurrence consists of an initial-state map $h_0:\Theta\to H$, transition maps $\Phi_t:\Theta\times H\to H$ for $t=1,\ldots,T$, and a scalar loss $\ell:\Theta\times H\to\mathbb R$, all continuously differentiable on open domains containing the evaluated inputs. Put $h_0=h_0(\theta)$, $h_t=\Phi_t(\theta,h_{t-1})$, and $J(\theta)=\ell(\theta,h_T)$. A fixed collection of external inputs, such as the observations at successive time steps, may be built into the maps $\Phi_t$. The same parameter vector $\theta$ is supplied to every transition.

For each map, its registered local derivative is the total derivative with respect to the indicated variable while the other displayed variables are held fixed. Transposes are Euclidean adjoints for the stated coordinate inner products. Evaluate and store $h_0,h_1,\ldots,h_T$. If a map is undefined, return DOMAIN_ERROR. For piecewise-$C^1$ maps, use only the derivative on a unique strict active branch; if any evaluated input lies on a branch boundary or has ambiguous branch selection, return NONDIFFERENTIABLE and no gradient.

Initialize $\lambda_T=(D_h\ell(\theta,h_T))^T$ and $g=(D_\theta\ell(\theta,h_T))^T$. For $t=T,T-1,\ldots,1$, set $A_t=D_\theta\Phi_t(\theta,h_{t-1})$, $B_t=D_h\Phi_t(\theta,h_{t-1})$, then update

$$g\leftarrow g+A_t^T\lambda_t,\qquad \lambda_{t-1}=B_t^T\lambda_t.$$

After the reverse sweep, set $g\leftarrow g+(Dh_0(\theta))^T\lambda_0$ and return $(J(\theta),g)$. For $T=0$, the reverse loop is empty and the same initialization and final update apply. All derivative calculations in this procedure are real-arithmetic operations on the specified maps; no floating-point accuracy guarantee is asserted.

The diagram shows the finite unrolling: arrows point from an input value to the operation that uses it. The single $\theta$ node feeds each transition and the loss, so the reverse updates add all of those contributions into the same accumulator $g$; the $h_t$ cotangents flow from right to left. The diagram illustrates the algorithm, and no correctness proof relies on it.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.55cm]
  \node[draw,circle] (theta) {$\theta$};
  \node[draw,rectangle,right of=theta,xshift=0.4cm] (h0) {$h_0(\theta)$};
  \node[draw,rectangle,right of=h0,xshift=0.65cm] (phi1) {$\Phi_1$};
  \node[draw,rectangle,right of=phi1,xshift=0.65cm] (h1) {$h_1$};
  \node[draw,rectangle,right of=h1,xshift=0.65cm] (phi2) {$\Phi_2$};
